  "interpretation": [
    "There is no automatic demand conclusion from lower work per task. At full CPU cost share and complete pass-through, elasticity above 1 creates more aggregate CPU work; below 1 creates less.",
    "When CPU is a small part of overall task cost, direct CPU savings reduce the task price much less than CPU work. Price-only rebound then requires much higher elasticity. Latency/quality improvements can add demand independently and are not estimated here.",
    "A premium CPU can create value even with flat or declining CPU unit count if it improves expensive GPU usage or customer responsiveness. The buyer may accept a higher chip price while lowering task TCO. The supplier captures only some of the benefit, depending on competing alternatives.",
    "Actual evidence does not estimate k, epsilon, a, utilization or supplier capture. Hence experimental CPU savings cannot be used to revise aggregate Intel units or ASP numerically."
  ]
